---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import SourceBuild from '@/components/SourceBuild.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import Waterfall from '@/components/Waterfall.astro'
---

<Doc
  title="Quickstart"
  description="Get mcpd running and see a trace from your own agent in about five minutes. One container, no database, no account."
  lede="Daemon running, agent instrumented, trace on screen. Nothing to sign up for and nothing to install alongside it."
  og="quickstart"
>
  <h2>1. Run the daemon</h2>
  <SourceBuild class="not-prose my-6" />
  <p>
    mcpd is one process with storage compiled in. There is no database to stand
    up and no configuration file to write.
  </p>

  <Command command="docker run -p 4317:4317 -v mcpd-data:/data ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd" />

  <p>
    The volume is optional but worth adding: without it, the traces go away when
    the container does. Prefer a binary?
    <a href="/docs/install" class="link">Install it directly</a>.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="what you should see"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'docker run -p 4317:4317 ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd' },
      { out: 'WARN listening on 0.0.0.0:4317 with open ingest. Anyone who can reach this port can write to it. Set --ingest-key (or MCPD_INGEST_KEYS) before exposing it.', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'WARN listening on 0.0.0.0:4317 with an open read API. Stored prompts and completions are readable by anyone who can reach this port. Set --read-key to require a key.', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'WARN listening on 0.0.0.0:4317 with an open run surface. Anyone who can reach this port can start runs and claim steps. Set --execute-key and --worker-key before exposing it.', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'INFO mcpd 0.1.0 listening on http://0.0.0.0:4317', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'INFO   interface   http://0.0.0.0:4317/' },
      { out: 'INFO   OTLP        http://0.0.0.0:4317/v1/traces' },
      { out: 'INFO   native      http://0.0.0.0:4317/v1/ingest' },
      { out: 'INFO   runs        http://0.0.0.0:4317/v1/api/runs' },
      { out: 'INFO   storage     sqlite at /data/mcpd.db' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The warnings come first, and they are deliberate. On your laptop they do
    not matter. Before this port is reachable by anything else, read
    <a href="/deploy" class="link">Deploy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Send it something</h2>
  <p>
    Two ways in, and you do not have to choose ours. If your agent already emits
    OpenTelemetry, point it at mcpd and write no new code at all:
  </p>

  <Command command="export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317" />

  <p>
    Otherwise install the SDK for your language. It has no required
    dependencies, and recording a span never blocks and never throws.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" region="init" title="connect" />

  <h2>3. Instrument a run</h2>
  <p>
    Wrap the work your agent does. A span records what went in, what came out,
    how long it took and what it cost; children nest inside it automatically, so
    a tool call made inside the block lands in the right place in the tree
    without being told where it belongs.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" region="trace" title="record a run" />

  <h2>4. Read it</h2>
  <p>
    Open <a href="http://localhost:4317" class="link" rel="noopener">localhost:4317</a>.
    Your run is in the list. Open it and you get the whole tree: every model
    call with its prompt and completion, every tool call with its arguments and
    result, timings, tokens and cost.
  </p>

  <Waterfall class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>5. Find the interesting one</h2>
  <p>
    The list is not where you will spend your time. Search is. mcpd indexes
    every prompt and completion, so you can go straight to the run where the
    model said the thing a customer complained about:
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" region="query" title="search and analyse" />

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <p>
    You now have the whole loop. The pages worth reading next depend on what you
    are doing:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Running this for other people:
      <a href="/deploy" class="link">Docker and Kubernetes</a>, then
      <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Watching a bill:
      <a href="/docs/cost" class="link">Cost accounting</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Measuring quality:
      <a href="/docs/evaluation" class="link">Scores and feedback</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Already have OpenTelemetry:
      <a href="/docs/opentelemetry" class="link">the OTLP path</a> in full.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Doc>
